window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2020-07-1", "source": "2020年07月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the use of translation apps. You can start your essay with the sentence \"The use of translation apps is becoming increasingly popular.\" You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "The use of translation apps is becoming increasingly popular. Whether traveling abroad, reading foreign\ndocuments, or chatting with friends from other countries, more and more people now reach for their phones\ninstead of a dictionary. This growing trend deserves a closer look.\nOn the one hand, these apps bring undeniable convenience, for they can translate a whole page in seconds,\nbreak down language barriers, and allow smooth communication across cultures. On the other hand, an over-\nreliance on them may weaken our motivation to learn foreign languages. If we let software do all the work, our\nown vocabulary and speaking ability may gradually fade.\nIn my view, translation apps should be treated as a helpful tool rather than a substitute for real language skills.\nWhile we enjoy their efficiency, we ought to keep practicing and improving ourselves. Only in this way can\ntechnology truly serve us instead of controlling us."}, "translation": {"prompt": "在中国，火锅已有2,000多年的历史，最早流行于最寒冷的地区，然后在很多地区盛行，出现了具有地方特色的种类。吃火锅时，家人和朋友围坐在桌边，桌子中间放着热腾腾的火锅。吃火锅时，人们可以根据自己的口味放肉、海鲜、蔬菜和其他配料，自己烹饪。人们可以一边尽情地聊天，一边享受美食。", "sample": "In China, hot pot has a history of more than 2,000 years. It first became popular in the coldest regions and then spread to many other areas, giving rise to varieties with distinct local features. When eating hot pot, family and friends sit around a table, with a steaming hot pot in the middle. They can add meat, seafood, vegetables and other ingredients according to their own tastes, and cook them themselves. Meanwhile, they can chat to their hearts' content while enjoying the food."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2020-07-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "\"Science and everyday life cannot and should not be separated.\" Those were the words uttered by pioneering British scientist Rosalind Franklin, who firmly believed that the pursuit of science should be  __26__  to all.\nAs a woman working in the first half of the 20th century, Franklin's contributions to some of the greatest scientific discoveries of our time including the structure of DNA were sadly  __27__  in her lifetime.\nMore than 60 years after Franklin's death, we are  __28__  living in a different world, where women play an important part in every echelon(阶层) of our society—not least in science, innovation, higher education and research. UK universities are world leaders when it comes to advancing and  __29__  gender equality.\nIn the past decade, we have seen a  __30__  increase in England in the number of women accepted on to full-time undergraduate degrees in science, technology, engineering and maths (Stem subjects). And in the last academic year, women  __31__  for more than half of all Stem postgraduates at UK universities.\nData shows us the  __32__  to success gets harder for women to climb the further up they go. Although women make up the majority of undergraduates in our universities, just under half of academic staff are female. At  __33__  levels, only a quarter of professors are women, and black women make up less than 2% of all female academic staff.\nThere are also stark differences in pay across grades. The gender pay gap based on median salaries across the sector in 2016-2017 was 13.7%,  __34__  there is still some way to go to ensure women are rising through the ranks to higher grade positions and being paid  __35__  .", "matchIntro": "How to Eat Well", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Why do so many Americans eat tons of processed food, the stuff that is correctly called junk(垃圾) and should really carry warning labels?"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It's not because fresh ingredients are hard to come by. Supermarkets offer more variety than ever, and there are over four times as many farmers markets in the US as there were 20 years ago. Nor is it for lack of available information. There are plenty of recipes(食谱), how-to videos and cooking classes available to anyone who has a computer, smartphone or television. If anything, the information is overwhelming."}, {"label": "C", "text": "And yet we aren't cooking. If you eat three meals a day and behave like most Americans, you probably get at least a third of your daily calories(卡路里) outside the home. Nearly two-thirds of us grab fast food once a week, and we get almost 25% of our daily calories from snacks. So we're eating out or taking in, and we don't sit down—or we do, but we hurry."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Shouldn't preparing—and consuming—food be a source of comfort, pride, health, well-being, relaxation, sociability? Something that connects us to other humans? Why would we want to outsource(外包) this basic task, especially when outsourcing it is so harmful?"}, {"label": "E", "text": "When I talk about cooking, I'm not talking about creating elaborate dinner parties or three-day science projects. I'm talking about simple, easy, everyday meals. My mission is to encourage green hands and those lacking time or memory to feed themselves. That means we need modest, realistic expectations, and we need to teach people to cook food that's good enough to share with family and friends."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Perhaps a return to real cooking needn't be far off. A recent Harris poll revealed that 79% of Americans say they enjoy cooking and 30% \"love it\"; 14% admit to not enjoying kitchen work and just 7% won't go near the stove at all. But this doesn't necessarily translate to real cooking, and the result of this survey shouldn't surprise anyone: 52% of those 65 or older cook at home five or more times per week; only a third of young people do."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Back in the 1950s most of us grew up in households where Mom cooked virtually every night. The intention to put a home-cooked meal on the table was pretty much universal. Most people couldn't afford to do otherwise."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Although frozen dinners were invented in the '40s, their popularity didn't boom until televisions became popular a decade or so later. Since then, packaged, pre-prepared meals have been what's for dinner. The microwave and fast-food chains were the biggest catalysts(催化剂), but the big food companies—which want to sell anything except the raw ingredients that go into cooking—made the home cook an endangered species."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Still, I find it strange that only a third of young people report preparing meals at home regularly. Isn't this the same crowd that rails against processed junk and champions craft cooking? And isn't this the generation who say they're concerned about their health and the well-being of the planet? If these are truly the values of many young people, then their behavior doesn't match their beliefs."}, {"label": "J", "text": "There have been half-hearted but well-publicized efforts by some food companies to reduce calories in their processed foods, but the Standard American Diet is still the polar opposite of the healthy, mostly plant-based diet that just about every expert says we should be eating. Considering that the government's standards are not nearly ambitious enough, the picture is clear: by not cooking at home, we're not eating the right things, and the consequences are hard to overstate."}, {"label": "K", "text": "To help quantify(量化) the costs of a poor diet, I recently tried to estimate this impact in terms of a most famous food, the burger(汉堡包). I concluded that the profit from burgers is more than offset by the damage they cause in health problems and environmental harm."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Cooking real food is the best defense—not to mention that any meal you're likely to eat at home contains about 200 fewer calories than one you would eat in a restaurant."}, {"label": "M", "text": "To those Americans for whom money is a concern, my advice is simple: Buy what you can afford, and cook it yourself. The common prescription is to primarily shop the grocery store, since that's where fresh produce, meat and seafood, and dairy are. And to save money and still eat well you don't need local, organic ingredients; all you need is real food. I'm not saying local food isn't better; it is. But there is plenty of decent food in the grocery stores."}, {"label": "N", "text": "The other sections you should get to know are the frozen foods and the canned goods. Frozen produce is still produce; canned tomatoes are still tomatoes. Just make sure you're getting real food without tons of added salt or sugar. Ask yourself, would Grandma consider this food? Does it look like something that might occur in nature? It's pretty much common sense: you want to buy food, not unidentifiable foodlike objects."}, {"label": "O", "text": "You don't have to hit the grocery store daily, nor do you need an abundance of skill. Since fewer than half of Americans say they cook at an intermediate level and only 20% describe their cooking skills as advanced, the crisis is one of confidence. And the only remedy for that is practice. There's nothing mysterious about cooking the evening meal. You just have to do a little thinking ahead and redefine what qualifies as dinner. Like any skill, cooking gets easier as you do it more: every time you cook, you advance your level of skills. Someday you won't even need recipes. My advice is that you not pay attention to the number of steps and ingredients, because they can be deceiving."}, {"label": "P", "text": "Time, I realize, is the biggest obstacle to cooking for most people. You must adjust your priorities to find time to cook. For instance, you can move a TV to the kitchen and watch your favorite shows while you're standing at the sink. No one is asking you to give up activities you like, but if you're watching food shows on TV, try cooking instead."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "The wallet is heading for extinction. As a day-to-day essential, it will die off with the generation who read print newspapers. The kind of shopping where you hand over notes and count out change in return now happens only in the most minor of our retail encounters, like buying a bar of chocolate or a pint of milk, from a corner shop. At the shops where you spend any real money, that money is increasingly abstracted. And this is more and more true, the higher up the scale you go. At the most cutting-edge retail stores Victoria Beckham on Dover Street, for instance you don't go and stand at any kind of cash register, when you decide to pay. The staff are equipped with iPads to take your payment while you relax on a sofa.\nWhich is nothing more or less than excellent service, if you have the money. But across society, the abstraction of the idea of cash makes me uneasy. Maybe I'm just old-fashioned. But earning money isn't quick or easy for most of us. Isn't it a bit weird that spending it should happen in half a blink(眨眼) of an eye? Doesn't a wallet—that time-honoured Friday-night feeling of pleasing, promising fatness—represent something that matters?\nBut I'll leave the economics to the experts. What bothers me about the death of the wallet is the change it represents in our physical environment. Everything about the look and feel of a wallet—the way the fastenings and materials wear and tear and loosen with age, the plastic and paper and gold and silver, and handwritten phone numbers and printed cinema tickets—is the very opposite of what our world is becoming. The opposite of a wallet is a smartphone or an iPad. The rounded edges, cool glass, smooth and unknowable as a pebble(鹅卵石). Instead of digging through pieces of paper and peering into corners, we move our fingers left and right. No more counting out coins. Show your wallet, if you still have one. It may not be here much longer.", "47": "The wallet is heading for extinction. As a day-to-day essential, it will die off with the generation who read print newspapers. The kind of shopping where you hand over notes and count out change in return now happens only in the most minor of our retail encounters, like buying a bar of chocolate or a pint of milk, from a corner shop. At the shops where you spend any real money, that money is increasingly abstracted. And this is more and more true, the higher up the scale you go. At the most cutting-edge retail stores Victoria Beckham on Dover Street, for instance you don't go and stand at any kind of cash register, when you decide to pay. The staff are equipped with iPads to take your payment while you relax on a sofa.\nWhich is nothing more or less than excellent service, if you have the money. But across society, the abstraction of the idea of cash makes me uneasy. Maybe I'm just old-fashioned. But earning money isn't quick or easy for most of us. Isn't it a bit weird that spending it should happen in half a blink(眨眼) of an eye? Doesn't a wallet—that time-honoured Friday-night feeling of pleasing, promising fatness—represent something that matters?\nBut I'll leave the economics to the experts. What bothers me about the death of the wallet is the change it represents in our physical environment. Everything about the look and feel of a wallet—the way the fastenings and materials wear and tear and loosen with age, the plastic and paper and gold and silver, and handwritten phone numbers and printed cinema tickets—is the very opposite of what our world is becoming. The opposite of a wallet is a smartphone or an iPad. The rounded edges, cool glass, smooth and unknowable as a pebble(鹅卵石). Instead of digging through pieces of paper and peering into corners, we move our fingers left and right. No more counting out coins. Show your wallet, if you still have one. It may not be here much longer.", "48": "The wallet is heading for extinction. As a day-to-day essential, it will die off with the generation who read print newspapers. The kind of shopping where you hand over notes and count out change in return now happens only in the most minor of our retail encounters, like buying a bar of chocolate or a pint of milk, from a corner shop. At the shops where you spend any real money, that money is increasingly abstracted. And this is more and more true, the higher up the scale you go. At the most cutting-edge retail stores Victoria Beckham on Dover Street, for instance you don't go and stand at any kind of cash register, when you decide to pay. The staff are equipped with iPads to take your payment while you relax on a sofa.\nWhich is nothing more or less than excellent service, if you have the money. But across society, the abstraction of the idea of cash makes me uneasy. Maybe I'm just old-fashioned. But earning money isn't quick or easy for most of us. Isn't it a bit weird that spending it should happen in half a blink(眨眼) of an eye? Doesn't a wallet—that time-honoured Friday-night feeling of pleasing, promising fatness—represent something that matters?\nBut I'll leave the economics to the experts. What bothers me about the death of the wallet is the change it represents in our physical environment. Everything about the look and feel of a wallet—the way the fastenings and materials wear and tear and loosen with age, the plastic and paper and gold and silver, and handwritten phone numbers and printed cinema tickets—is the very opposite of what our world is becoming. The opposite of a wallet is a smartphone or an iPad. The rounded edges, cool glass, smooth and unknowable as a pebble(鹅卵石). Instead of digging through pieces of paper and peering into corners, we move our fingers left and right. No more counting out coins. Show your wallet, if you still have one. It may not be here much longer.", "49": "The wallet is heading for extinction. As a day-to-day essential, it will die off with the generation who read print newspapers. The kind of shopping where you hand over notes and count out change in return now happens only in the most minor of our retail encounters, like buying a bar of chocolate or a pint of milk, from a corner shop. At the shops where you spend any real money, that money is increasingly abstracted. And this is more and more true, the higher up the scale you go. At the most cutting-edge retail stores Victoria Beckham on Dover Street, for instance you don't go and stand at any kind of cash register, when you decide to pay. The staff are equipped with iPads to take your payment while you relax on a sofa.\nWhich is nothing more or less than excellent service, if you have the money. But across society, the abstraction of the idea of cash makes me uneasy. Maybe I'm just old-fashioned. But earning money isn't quick or easy for most of us. Isn't it a bit weird that spending it should happen in half a blink(眨眼) of an eye? Doesn't a wallet—that time-honoured Friday-night feeling of pleasing, promising fatness—represent something that matters?\nBut I'll leave the economics to the experts. What bothers me about the death of the wallet is the change it represents in our physical environment. Everything about the look and feel of a wallet—the way the fastenings and materials wear and tear and loosen with age, the plastic and paper and gold and silver, and handwritten phone numbers and printed cinema tickets—is the very opposite of what our world is becoming. The opposite of a wallet is a smartphone or an iPad. The rounded edges, cool glass, smooth and unknowable as a pebble(鹅卵石). Instead of digging through pieces of paper and peering into corners, we move our fingers left and right. No more counting out coins. Show your wallet, if you still have one. It may not be here much longer.", "50": "The wallet is heading for extinction. As a day-to-day essential, it will die off with the generation who read print newspapers. The kind of shopping where you hand over notes and count out change in return now happens only in the most minor of our retail encounters, like buying a bar of chocolate or a pint of milk, from a corner shop. At the shops where you spend any real money, that money is increasingly abstracted. And this is more and more true, the higher up the scale you go. At the most cutting-edge retail stores Victoria Beckham on Dover Street, for instance you don't go and stand at any kind of cash register, when you decide to pay. The staff are equipped with iPads to take your payment while you relax on a sofa.\nWhich is nothing more or less than excellent service, if you have the money. But across society, the abstraction of the idea of cash makes me uneasy. Maybe I'm just old-fashioned. But earning money isn't quick or easy for most of us. Isn't it a bit weird that spending it should happen in half a blink(眨眼) of an eye? Doesn't a wallet—that time-honoured Friday-night feeling of pleasing, promising fatness—represent something that matters?\nBut I'll leave the economics to the experts. What bothers me about the death of the wallet is the change it represents in our physical environment. Everything about the look and feel of a wallet—the way the fastenings and materials wear and tear and loosen with age, the plastic and paper and gold and silver, and handwritten phone numbers and printed cinema tickets—is the very opposite of what our world is becoming. The opposite of a wallet is a smartphone or an iPad. The rounded edges, cool glass, smooth and unknowable as a pebble(鹅卵石). Instead of digging through pieces of paper and peering into corners, we move our fingers left and right. No more counting out coins. Show your wallet, if you still have one. It may not be here much longer.", "51": "It's late in the evening: time to close the book and turn off the computer. You're done for the day. What you may not realize, however, is that the learning process actually continues—in your dreams.\nIt might sound like science fiction, but researchers are increasingly focusing on the relationship between the knowledge and skills our brains absorb during the day and the fragmented, often bizarre imaginings they generate at night. Scientists have found that dreaming about a task we've learned is associated with improved performance in that activity (suggesting that there's some truth to the popular notion that we're \"getting\" a foreign language once we begin dreaming in it). What's more, researchers are coming to recognize that dreaming is an essential part of understanding, organizing and retaining what we learn.\nWhile we sleep, research indicates, the brain replays the patterns of activity it experienced during waking hours, allowing us to enter what one psychologist calls a neural(神经的) virtual reality. A vivid example of such replay can be seen in a video researchers made recently about sleep disorders. They taught a series of dance moves to a group of patients with conditions like sleepwalking, in which the sleeper engages in the kind of physical movement that does not normally occur during sleep. They then videotaped the subjects as they slept. Lying in bed, eyes closed, one female patient on the tape performs the dance moves she learned earlier.\nThis shows that while our bodies are at rest, our brains are drawing what's important from the information and events we've recently encountered, then integrating that data into the vast store of what we already know. In a 2010 study, researchers at Harvard Medical School reported that college students who dreamed about a computer maze(迷宫) task they had learned showed a 10-fold improvement in their ability to find their way through the maze compared with participants who did not dream about the task.\nRobert Stickgold, one of the Harvard researchers, suggests that studying right before bedtime or taking a nap following a study session in the afternoon might increase the odds of dreaming about the material. Think about that as your head hits the pillow tonight.", "52": "It's late in the evening: time to close the book and turn off the computer. You're done for the day. What you may not realize, however, is that the learning process actually continues—in your dreams.\nIt might sound like science fiction, but researchers are increasingly focusing on the relationship between the knowledge and skills our brains absorb during the day and the fragmented, often bizarre imaginings they generate at night. Scientists have found that dreaming about a task we've learned is associated with improved performance in that activity (suggesting that there's some truth to the popular notion that we're \"getting\" a foreign language once we begin dreaming in it). What's more, researchers are coming to recognize that dreaming is an essential part of understanding, organizing and retaining what we learn.\nWhile we sleep, research indicates, the brain replays the patterns of activity it experienced during waking hours, allowing us to enter what one psychologist calls a neural(神经的) virtual reality. A vivid example of such replay can be seen in a video researchers made recently about sleep disorders. They taught a series of dance moves to a group of patients with conditions like sleepwalking, in which the sleeper engages in the kind of physical movement that does not normally occur during sleep. They then videotaped the subjects as they slept. Lying in bed, eyes closed, one female patient on the tape performs the dance moves she learned earlier.\nThis shows that while our bodies are at rest, our brains are drawing what's important from the information and events we've recently encountered, then integrating that data into the vast store of what we already know. In a 2010 study, researchers at Harvard Medical School reported that college students who dreamed about a computer maze(迷宫) task they had learned showed a 10-fold improvement in their ability to find their way through the maze compared with participants who did not dream about the task.\nRobert Stickgold, one of the Harvard researchers, suggests that studying right before bedtime or taking a nap following a study session in the afternoon might increase the odds of dreaming about the material. Think about that as your head hits the pillow tonight.", "53": "It's late in the evening: time to close the book and turn off the computer. You're done for the day. What you may not realize, however, is that the learning process actually continues—in your dreams.\nIt might sound like science fiction, but researchers are increasingly focusing on the relationship between the knowledge and skills our brains absorb during the day and the fragmented, often bizarre imaginings they generate at night. Scientists have found that dreaming about a task we've learned is associated with improved performance in that activity (suggesting that there's some truth to the popular notion that we're \"getting\" a foreign language once we begin dreaming in it). What's more, researchers are coming to recognize that dreaming is an essential part of understanding, organizing and retaining what we learn.\nWhile we sleep, research indicates, the brain replays the patterns of activity it experienced during waking hours, allowing us to enter what one psychologist calls a neural(神经的) virtual reality. A vivid example of such replay can be seen in a video researchers made recently about sleep disorders. They taught a series of dance moves to a group of patients with conditions like sleepwalking, in which the sleeper engages in the kind of physical movement that does not normally occur during sleep. They then videotaped the subjects as they slept. Lying in bed, eyes closed, one female patient on the tape performs the dance moves she learned earlier.\nThis shows that while our bodies are at rest, our brains are drawing what's important from the information and events we've recently encountered, then integrating that data into the vast store of what we already know. In a 2010 study, researchers at Harvard Medical School reported that college students who dreamed about a computer maze(迷宫) task they had learned showed a 10-fold improvement in their ability to find their way through the maze compared with participants who did not dream about the task.\nRobert Stickgold, one of the Harvard researchers, suggests that studying right before bedtime or taking a nap following a study session in the afternoon might increase the odds of dreaming about the material. Think about that as your head hits the pillow tonight.", "54": "It's late in the evening: time to close the book and turn off the computer. You're done for the day. What you may not realize, however, is that the learning process actually continues—in your dreams.\nIt might sound like science fiction, but researchers are increasingly focusing on the relationship between the knowledge and skills our brains absorb during the day and the fragmented, often bizarre imaginings they generate at night. Scientists have found that dreaming about a task we've learned is associated with improved performance in that activity (suggesting that there's some truth to the popular notion that we're \"getting\" a foreign language once we begin dreaming in it). What's more, researchers are coming to recognize that dreaming is an essential part of understanding, organizing and retaining what we learn.\nWhile we sleep, research indicates, the brain replays the patterns of activity it experienced during waking hours, allowing us to enter what one psychologist calls a neural(神经的) virtual reality. A vivid example of such replay can be seen in a video researchers made recently about sleep disorders. They taught a series of dance moves to a group of patients with conditions like sleepwalking, in which the sleeper engages in the kind of physical movement that does not normally occur during sleep. They then videotaped the subjects as they slept. Lying in bed, eyes closed, one female patient on the tape performs the dance moves she learned earlier.\nThis shows that while our bodies are at rest, our brains are drawing what's important from the information and events we've recently encountered, then integrating that data into the vast store of what we already know. In a 2010 study, researchers at Harvard Medical School reported that college students who dreamed about a computer maze(迷宫) task they had learned showed a 10-fold improvement in their ability to find their way through the maze compared with participants who did not dream about the task.\nRobert Stickgold, one of the Harvard researchers, suggests that studying right before bedtime or taking a nap following a study session in the afternoon might increase the odds of dreaming about the material. Think about that as your head hits the pillow tonight.", "55": "It's late in the evening: time to close the book and turn off the computer. You're done for the day. What you may not realize, however, is that the learning process actually continues—in your dreams.\nIt might sound like science fiction, but researchers are increasingly focusing on the relationship between the knowledge and skills our brains absorb during the day and the fragmented, often bizarre imaginings they generate at night. Scientists have found that dreaming about a task we've learned is associated with improved performance in that activity (suggesting that there's some truth to the popular notion that we're \"getting\" a foreign language once we begin dreaming in it). What's more, researchers are coming to recognize that dreaming is an essential part of understanding, organizing and retaining what we learn.\nWhile we sleep, research indicates, the brain replays the patterns of activity it experienced during waking hours, allowing us to enter what one psychologist calls a neural(神经的) virtual reality. A vivid example of such replay can be seen in a video researchers made recently about sleep disorders. They taught a series of dance moves to a group of patients with conditions like sleepwalking, in which the sleeper engages in the kind of physical movement that does not normally occur during sleep. They then videotaped the subjects as they slept. Lying in bed, eyes closed, one female patient on the tape performs the dance moves she learned earlier.\nThis shows that while our bodies are at rest, our brains are drawing what's important from the information and events we've recently encountered, then integrating that data into the vast store of what we already know. In a 2010 study, researchers at Harvard Medical School reported that college students who dreamed about a computer maze(迷宫) task they had learned showed a 10-fold improvement in their ability to find their way through the maze compared with participants who did not dream about the task.\nRobert Stickgold, one of the Harvard researchers, suggests that studying right before bedtime or taking a nap following a study session in the afternoon might increase the odds of dreaming about the material. Think about that as your head hits the pillow tonight."}, "questions": [{"id": "2020-07-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What were people in parts of Scotland advised to do?", "options": ["A) Watch the weather forecast.", "B) Evacuate the area with the orange alert.", "C) Avoid travel on Wednesday.", "D) Prepare enough food and drink."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Avoid travel on Wednesday.\n【定位】The transport minister advised people in parts of Scotland covered by the orange alert to\navoid travel on Wednesday.\n交通大臣建议橙色预警覆盖区域内的苏格兰民众，周三不要出行。\n【信号】建议动词 advised 直接领出动作。信号是建议动词本身——录音里 The transport\nminister advised people in parts of Scotland covered by the orange alert to avoid\ntravel on Wednesday. 中 advised…to 后面跟的 avoid travel on Wednesday 就是被建议\n做的事，听到 advised 立刻锁定它后面的动词短语。\n【替换】advised people in parts of Scotland covered by the orange alert to avoid travel on Wednesday ↔\nAvoid travel on Wednesday.\n几乎原句裁剪，去掉对象状语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头说 heavy snow is forecast across the country，出现了 forecast 一词，A\nWatch the weather forecast. 就靠\"看天气预报\"这个原词诱你选；但录音从没建议民众去\n看预报，是答非所问。\n借用原文：heavy snow is forecast across the country\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音多次出现 Orange warnings are in force for many areas 和 orange alert，B\nEvacuate the area with the orange alert. 借\"橙色预警/区域\"这些原词制造熟悉感；但录\n音只让\"avoid travel\"（别出行），从没说\"evacuate\"（撤离），动作被偷换了。\n借用原文：Orange warnings are in force for many areas\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音后半对司机说 Drivers should make sure they're prepared for their journey with\nwarm clothing, food and drink，出现了 food and drink，D Prepare enough food and\ndrink. 就盗这组词；但这条是对\"司机\"说的、且本题问的是\"民众\"被建议做什么，对象和位\n置都错了。\n借用原文：Drivers should make sure they're prepared for their journey with warm clothing, food\nand drink", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the head of road policing urge drivers to do?", "options": ["A) Pay more attention to the roads.", "B) Stay at a safer place.", "C) Bring more mobile phones.", "D) Take a train home."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Pay more attention to the roads.\n【定位】The head of road policing said, \"I would urge drivers to take extra care on the roads for\ntheir journeys.\"\n道路警务负责人说：\"我要敦促司机们在出行途中格外注意路况。\"\n【信号】催促动词 urge 紧领核心动作。信号是 I would urge drivers to take extra care on the\nroads 里的 urge——题干用的就是 urge，录音原话也用 urge，词后接的 take extra care\non the roads 就是答案落点，听到 urge 立刻锁其后。\n【替换】take extra care on the roads ↔ Pay more attention to the roads.\n同义替换：take care=pay attention\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n录音末句 consider if you really need to travel when conditions are this poor 让人联\n想\"找更安全的地方\"，B Stay at a safer place. 借这股\"安全\"语感诱选；但 urge 后真正的\n动作是\"take care on the roads\"（在路上当心），不是\"待在更安全处\"，是过度推断。\n借用原文：consider if you really need to travel when conditions are this poor\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音对司机说 a charged mobile phone（带一部充好电的手机），C Bring more mobile\nphones. 盗 mobile phone 原词并把\"一部充电的\"偷改成\"多带几部\"；不仅数量错，这也不\n是 urge 那句的核心动作，是答非所问。\n借用原文：a charged mobile phone\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说 Trains, planes and ferries are also likely to be affected，出现了 trains，D Take\na train home. 借\"火车\"原词诱选；但录音是讲\"火车也会受影响\"，根本没催司机坐火车回\n家，与 urge 那句无关。\n借用原文：Trains, planes and ferries are also likely to be affected\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Romania's wetlands are coming back to life thanks to help from local communities, the World Wildlife\nFund, and funding from the European Union. Roughly 2,224 acres of the picturesque Danube Delta—\nhome to 300 species of birds—have been filled with wildlife. The land has been connected to the\nnetwork of lakes and streams in the area.\n在当地社区、世界自然基金会的帮助以及欧盟的资助下,罗马尼亚的湿地正在恢复生机。风景如画的多瑙河\n三角洲约2224英亩的土地——这里是300种鸟类的栖息地——已经充满了野生动物。这片土地已与该地\n区的湖泊和溪流网络连接起来。\n2 \"A lot of birds have migrated to the area, and one doesn't need to travel long hours anymore or go to\nother lakes to watch the birds,\" says Ion Meuta, Deputy Mayor of Mahmudia.\nMahmudia副市长Ion Meuta说:\"许多鸟类已经迁徙到这个地区,人们不再需要长途跋涉或去其他湖泊观\n鸟了。\"\n3 The area around Mahmudia, which is a UNESCO World Heritage site, is the third-most bio-diverse in\nthe world, after Australia's Great Barrier Reef and the Galapagos Islands. Groups used earth-moving\nequipment to restore the waterways. Government officials proposed to protect the area's endangered wild\nfish by issuing a fishing ban over the next decade.\nMahmudia周边地区是联合国教科文组织世界遗产地,是世界上生物多样性第三丰富的地区,仅次于澳大利\n亚大堡礁和加拉帕戈斯群岛。各团体使用土方设备恢复了水道。政府官员提议在未来十年内实施捕鱼禁令,\n以保护该地区濒危的野生鱼类。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What is the news report mainly about?", "options": ["A) There is only one ecosystem in Europe.", "B) Romania's wetlands thrive again.", "C) The wildlife in Romania isn't well protected.", "D) There are 200 species of birds in Romania's wetlands."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） Romania's wetlands thrive again.\n【定位】Romania's wetlands are coming back to life thanks to help from local communities, the World\nWildlife Fund, and funding from the European Union.\n在当地社区、世界自然基金会以及欧盟资金的帮助下，罗马尼亚的湿地正在重新焕发生机。\n【信号】开头点题句定主旨。新闻的主旨通常落在第一句导语。Romania's wetlands are coming\nback to life 开门见山点出整条消息的核心——罗马尼亚湿地正在复苏；后文的鸟类迁入、\n水道修复、禁渔提议都是围绕这一核心展开的细节。主旨题就锁这句开头的总述句。\n【替换】coming back to life ↔ thrive again\n复苏 = 再度繁荣\nRomania's wetlands ↔ Romania's wetlands\n主体同为罗马尼亚湿地\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音从没说欧洲\"只有一个生态系统\"，There is only one ecosystem in Europe. 是凭空捏\n造，且 \"only one\" 这种绝对说法与新闻在讲某地恢复的事实毫不相干。\nC) 与原文相反\n录音盗用了 wildlife 这个词，The wildlife in Romania isn't well protected. 借它说\"野生\n动物没被保护好\"，但全篇是说湿地恢复、动物回归、政府还提出禁渔保护，方向恰恰相\n反，是把好消息说反了。\n借用原文：filled with wildlife\nD) 原词陷阱\nThere are 200 species of birds in Romania's wetlands. 盗用了录音的 \"species of birds\"\n制造熟悉感，但它只是篇中一个枝节数字，主旨题不能选细节项；何况录音明确说的是\nhome to 300 species of birds——300 种，不是 200 种，数字也被改错了。\n借用原文：home to 300 species of birds", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What did government officials propose to do to protect the endangered fish?", "options": ["A) Block the waterways.", "B) Restore the fishing ban.", "C) Use monitoring equipment.", "D) Prohibit fishing in the next 10 years."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Prohibit fishing in the next 10 years.\n【定位】Government officials proposed to protect the area's endangered wild fish by issuing a fishing\nban over the next decade.\n政府官员提议，通过在未来十年颁布禁渔令来保护该地区濒危的野生鱼类。\n【信号】提出做某事的动作句。题干问\"官员提出做什么\"，定位就盯表\"提出\"的动词。Government\nofficials proposed to protect…by issuing a fishing ban over the next decade 里的\n\"proposed…by issuing a fishing ban\" 正是那个动作句，by 后面跟的\"颁布禁渔令\"就是具\n体做法，答案落在这里。\n【替换】issuing a fishing ban ↔ Prohibit fishing\n禁渔令 = 禁止捕鱼\nover the next decade ↔ in the next 10 years\ndecade = 10 年\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nBlock the waterways. 盗用了录音里的 \"waterways\"，但录音说的是用设备去 restore the\nwaterways（修复水道），方向是修复、不是 block（阻断）；而且这是修复水道的动作，\n不是保护鱼的措施，把别处的动作安到本题上了。\n借用原文：restore the waterways\nB) 原词陷阱\nRestore the fishing ban. 几乎照搬录音原词 fishing ban，极易\"听到就选\"；但录音是\nissuing a fishing ban（新颁布一项禁渔令），不是 restore（恢复一项原有的禁渔令）\n——动词 issue 被偷换成 restore，意思变了。\n借用原文：issuing a fishing ban\nC) 答非所问\nUse monitoring equipment. 盗用了录音里的 \"equipment\"，但录音说的是 Groups used\nearth-moving equipment to restore the waterways——是各团体用\"挖土设备\"修水道，\n既不是 monitoring（监测）设备，也不是官员保护鱼提出的措施，与本题问的事对不上。\n借用原文：used earth-moving equipment to restore the waterways", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What happened to Scott a month ago?", "options": ["A) He had a car accident.", "B) He attended his graduation ceremony.", "C) He had a heart attack.", "D) He gave a performance in the auditorium."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He had a car accident.\n【定位】Scott Dunn recently awoke from a medically-induced sleep he'd been in after a car accident.\n斯科特·邓恩最近从一次医学诱导的昏睡中醒来,此前他因一场车祸而陷入昏睡。\n【信号】开篇即点核心事件。新闻开篇第一句就交代主人公遭遇,awoke from a medically-induced\nsleep he'd been in after a car accident 直接点出 car accident 这一核心事件,新闻\"开门\n见山报事件\"的特点使首句成为答案句。\n【替换】after a car accident ↔ He had a car accident.\n几乎原句对应\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nHe attended his graduation ceremony. 盗用了反复出现的原词 graduation;但题问 a\nmonth ago 时他出了车祸、恰恰\"错过\"了原定典礼(he'd missed his high school\ngraduation),并未在一月前参加典礼,属答非所问。\n借用原文：he'd missed his high school graduation\nC) 未提及\nHe had a heart attack. 与正确事件\"车祸\"同属突发坏事、听感都急促,极易混;但原文只字未\n提心脏病,他遭遇的是 a car accident,此项把事件换成了原文没说的另一种。\nD) 原词陷阱\nHe gave a performance in the auditorium. 盗用了原文真出现的地点词 auditorium;但原\n文 auditorium 是同学们聚集补办典礼的礼堂,从无他\"表演\"一说,属借原词无中生有。\n借用原文：met in the school's auditorium for graduation", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What did Scott ask about when he regained consciousness?", "options": ["A) What happened to him.", "B) What date it was.", "C) When the graduation ceremony was.", "D) Where he was."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） What date it was.\n【定位】\"I remember waking up in the hospital and asking mom what date it was. She told me it was\nthe 28th,\" he said.\n\"我记得在医院醒来,问妈妈那天是几号。她告诉我是28号,\"他说。\n【信号】他亲口说出的问话。答案句是他本人的直接引语 asking mom what date it was,动词\nasking 后面紧跟的 what date it was 就是他问的内容,人物原话里\"问了什么\"即答案信号。\n【替换】asking mom what date it was ↔ What date it was.\n问话内容原样对应\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nWhat happened to him. 像是承接\"出了车祸\"的合理联想,但题干问的是他苏醒后\"问了什\n么\",原文他问的是 what date it was,并没有问\"自己发生了什么\",此项答错了方向。\nC) 原词陷阱\nWhen the graduation ceremony was. 盗用了高频原词 graduation,又和正确项同样关\n乎\"时间\",极易混;但他问的是 date(几号),不是\"典礼在何时\",原文中典礼时间是后文交代、并\n非他苏醒时所问。\n借用原文：met in the school's auditorium for graduation\nD) 未提及\nWhere he was. 把疑问维度换成\"地点\";苏醒后问自己身处何地虽是常见桥段,但原文他问的\n是日期 what date it was,从未问 where,此项是原文没出现的内容。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why was Scott speechless at the graduation ceremony?", "options": ["A) He was really touched by his classmates.", "B) He didn't know what happened at all.", "C) He couldn't remember what to say.", "D) His parents wore caps and gowns."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He was really touched by his classmates.\n【定位】\"I'm speechless,\" Scott said. \"I don't know how to even explain it. I'm speechless to know that\nso many people are behind me.\"\n\"我说不出话来,\"斯科特说。\"我都不知道该怎么形容。得知有这么多人支持我,我激动得说不\n出话。\"\n【信号】结尾本人道出缘由。答案句在新闻收尾处,他自己点明 I'm speechless to know that so\nmany people are behind me,speechless 后面的 to know that… 正是\"说不出话\"的原因\n——这么多人支持他,本人解释句即答案信号。\n【替换】so many people are behind me ↔ He was really touched by his classmates.\n概括:被众人支持=被同学感动\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nHe didn't know what happened at all. 借用了原文 I don't know how to even explain it\n里的 don't know 字样;但他说的是\"不知如何形容(激动)\",并非\"完全不知道发生了什么\",事实\n上他很清楚同学为他补办了典礼,此项把意思扭反了。\n借用原文：I don't know how to even explain it\nC) 偷换概念\nHe couldn't remember what to say. 与 speechless/\"说不出话\"听感极近,最具迷惑性;但\nspeechless 在此是\"激动得说不出话\",原因是被支持感动,而非\"想不起该说什么\",二者并非一\n回事。\nD) 原词陷阱\nHis parents wore caps and gowns. 盗用了原文确实念过的画面词 caps and gowns;但原\n文穿学位帽袍的是 Students(同学),不是他父母,且这画面与\"他为何说不出话\"无关,既张冠又\n答非所问。\n借用原文：Students wore their caps and gowns and sat in the front of the auditorium", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman say is the disadvantage of working from home?", "options": ["A) Her children's disruption.", "B) Quiet atmosphere.", "C) A sense of isolation.", "D) Longer working hours."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A sense of isolation.\n【定位】I find isolation challenging at times. Apart from my mother, not too many people I know have\ntime for phone chats during working hours.\n我有时觉得孤立很难熬。除了我妈妈，我认识的人里没几个在工作时间有空跟我闲聊。\n【信号】被问者直接应答。男士先抛出 Do you enjoy working from home?，女士在回答里直说 I\nfind isolation challenging at times.，这句被问者的直接应答就是答案信号。\n【替换】I find isolation challenging at times ↔ A sense of isolation.\n同根词换说法\n【排除】A)\n全文女士只提到 Apart from my mother，从未说孩子打扰，Her children's disruption. 是\n凭居家办公的常识脑补出来的，原文无据。\nB) 原词陷阱\nQuiet atmosphere. 盗用了原文 I find the library a bit too quiet. 里的 quiet，但那是男\n士评价图书馆，不是女士说的居家办公弊端，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I find the library a bit too quiet.\nD)\nLonger working hours. 也是居家办公常被吐槽的点，但女士全程没提工时长短，属凭印象\n添加的未提及项。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the man say is counterproductive to getting work done in the library?", "options": ["A) It doesn't offer coffee.", "B) It's too quiet.", "C) It doesn't have free Wi-Fi.", "D) It lacks the materials he needs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It's too quiet.\n【定位】I find the library a bit too quiet. I'd lack inspiration or stimulation, which would be\ncounterproductive to getting a lot of work done.\n我觉得图书馆有点太安静了。我会缺少灵感或刺激，这会对完成大量工作起反作用。\n【信号】因果链指向起因。男士说太安静会导致 lack inspiration or stimulation, which would be\ncounterproductive，counterproductive 的源头顺着 which 往前推就是 too quiet，因果\n链就是答案信号。\n【替换】I find the library a bit too quiet ↔ It's too quiet.\n近乎原句压缩\n【排除】A)\nIt doesn't offer coffee. 借了后文咖啡馆话题里的 coffee 来诱选，但男士说图书馆的问题是\n太安静，从没说图书馆不供咖啡，答非所问。\n借用原文：Coffee shops seem to have effectively become office spaces\nC)\nIt doesn't have free Wi-Fi. 原文从未提及 Wi-Fi，是利用办公缺网络这一常识硬造的未提及\n项。\nD)\nIt lacks the materials he needs. 把男士说的缺 inspiration or stimulation 灵感刺激，偷\n换成缺实体资料，概念被换了。\n借用原文：I'd lack inspiration or stimulation", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the woman like about doing one's work in a café?", "options": ["A) The sense of being out in the world.", "B) The coffee it provides.", "C) The coffee table.", "D) The comfortable working conditions."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The sense of being out in the world.\n【定位】I love the noise, the people, the busyness of the café, the sense of being out in the world.\n我喜欢那种喧闹、人来人往、咖啡馆的忙碌，还有那种置身世界之中的感觉。\n【信号】I love 喜爱信号。女士用 I love the noise, the people, the busyness 直接亮出喜爱信号，\nI love 后面一串并列就是她喜欢的点，是最明确的答案信号。\n【替换】the sense of being out in the world ↔ The sense of being out in the world.\n原句直接命中\n【排除】B)\nThe coffee it provides. 蹭的是咖啡馆 café 这个场景词，但女士 I love 的清单里是噪音、\n人群、忙碌，根本没提咖啡本身，答非所问。\n借用原文：the busyness of the café\nC)\nThe coffee table. 利用 coffee 一词的近形干扰，原文从未出现 coffee table，是凭咖啡馆\n联想硬造的未提及项。\nD)\nThe comfortable working conditions. 把咖啡馆想象成舒适的办公环境，但女士喜欢的是\n热闹忙碌，原文未提舒适，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What is most annoying for café owners according to the woman?", "options": ["A) People don't order anything.", "B) People bring their laptops and paperwork.", "C) People occupy valuable table space in quiet times.", "D) People of two occupy a table for six."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） People of two occupy a table for six.\n【定位】The most annoying ones for café owners must surely be those usually only two of them who\noccupy a table for six with their laptops and paperwork.\n对咖啡馆老板来说，最招人烦的肯定是那些通常只有两个人却占着一张六人桌、摆满笔记本\n电脑和文件的人。\n【信号】the most 最高级。女士用 The most annoying ones for café owners must surely be 这\n个最高级加 surely 强调，直接把最招烦的圈定为后面那种人，最高级就是答案信号。\n【替换】only two of them who occupy a table for six ↔ People of two occupy a table for six.\n原句改写为选项\n【排除】A)\nPeople don't order anything. 盗用了原文 not order much，但那只是女士先提的一般性\n烦恼，没被 the most 修饰，不点单不是最招烦的那条。\n借用原文：spend a lot of time there and not order much\nB)\nPeople bring their laptops and paperwork. 直接搬了原文 with their laptops and\npaperwork，但带电脑文件只是六人桌那种人的修饰细节，本身不是被点名最招烦的行为，\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：occupy a table for six with their laptops and paperwork\nC)\nPeople occupy valuable table space in quiet times. 盗用了末段 take up the valuable\ntable space，但原文说占用宝贵桌位是在 busy periods，并非 quiet times，时段被说\n反。\n借用原文：they can take up the valuable table space in busy periods", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What makes the woman think about changing her job?", "options": ["A) She is not satisfied with the salary.", "B) She is not capable of the job.", "C) She often works overtime.", "D) She's received a job offer from another company."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She is not satisfied with the salary.\n【定位】And I really think my skills and abilities deserve a higher salary.\n而且我真心觉得，凭我的技能和能力，理应拿到更高的薪水。\n【信号】I really think 主观加重语气标出真实理由。她在 I really think my skills and abilities\ndeserve a higher salary 里用 I really think 加重语气，亮出最直接的换工作动因——觉得\n自己值更高的薪水，这种当事人强调式的自述句就是原因题的答案句。\n【替换】my skills and abilities deserve a higher salary ↔ not satisfied with the salary\n\"理应拿更高薪\"=\"对现薪不满\"的换说法\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n原文里她说 my skills and abilities deserve a higher salary，是在标榜自己能力强、配更\n高薪，选项却说她 not capable of the job，把意思反了过来。\n借用原文：my skills and abilities deserve a higher salary\nC) 未提及\n对话从头到尾在谈薪水、晋升、忠诚，从没提到她\"常加班\"，She often works overtime.\n是凭\"职场\"话题想当然补出来的，原文无据。\nD) 与原文相反\n她说的是 I'm going to start looking for another job——还没开始找，更没拿到 offer，\n选项 She's received a job offer from another company. 把\"准备去找\"夸成\"已经拿到\noffer\"，与原文不符。\n借用原文：I'm going to start looking for another job", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the man say about people who keep changing their jobs?", "options": ["A) They may be considered as less loyal.", "B) They won't get the promotion opportunities.", "C) They should take more responsibility at work.", "D) They will be given hiring priority."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They may be considered as less loyal.\n【定位】If you do several jobs in a relatively short span of time, prospective employers might perceive\nthat you lack loyalty.\n如果你在相对短的时间里做了好几份工作，未来的雇主可能会觉得你缺乏忠诚度。\n【信号】被问方男方的直接论断句。题目问\"男方怎么说\"，答案就落在男方那段 prospective\nemployers might perceive that you lack loyalty——这是男方针对频繁换工作者下的直\n接判断，正是该题答案句。\n【替换】might perceive that you lack loyalty ↔ considered as less loyal\nperceive…lack loyalty = considered as less loyal\n【排除】B) 未提及\n男方只说雇主会觉得你不忠诚、因而 reluctant to employ you，谈的是\"难被录用\"，并没\n说\"得不到晋升机会\"，They won't get the promotion opportunities. 在男方这段里无\n据。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey wanted you to take on more work and responsibility 里的 more responsibility\n确实被念到了，但那是男方在转述女方旧东家的做法，不是男方\"对频繁换工作者\"的说法，\n选项 They should take more responsibility at work. 盗了 responsibility 一词张冠李戴。\n借用原文：They wanted you to take on more work and responsibility\nD) 与原文相反\n男方的判断是雇主会 wary and reluctant to employ you(迟疑、不愿录用)，方向是负面\n的；选项 They will be given hiring priority. 却说成\"被优先录用\"，把极性彻底反了。\n借用原文：wary and reluctant to employ you", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the woman say would happen even if she got promoted in her current company?", "options": ["A) She might have to do extra work every day.", "B) She might not get a pay rise.", "C) She might not get enough vacation.", "D) She might not gain more experience."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She might not get a pay rise.\n【定位】And even if I get a promotion, I'm not guaranteed to get a raise.\n而且就算我升了职，也不一定能涨薪。\n【信号】even if 让步句，转折后才是真意。她在 even if I get a promotion, I'm not guaranteed\nto get a raise 用 even if 设让步——\"升职\"是铺垫，让步后的\"不一定涨薪\"才是她要表达的\n代价，正是答案句。\n【替换】not guaranteed to get a raise ↔ might not get a pay rise\nnot guaranteed a raise = might not get a pay rise\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\ntake on more work and responsibility 里的 more work 确被念到，但那是男方追问女\n方\"过去那家公司\"的情形，不是\"在现公司升职后\"的后果；且选项 She might have to do\nextra work every day. 多出 every day，原文从未说\"每天\"，属夸大。\n借用原文：They wanted you to take on more work and responsibility\nC) 未提及\n全程谈的是薪水、晋升、忠诚、经验，从没涉及 vacation，She might not get enough\nvacation. 在原文中毫无依据。\nD) 与原文相反\n经验在原文里是女方的正面收获 I've gathered a lot of experience，并非\"升职后得不\n到\"的坏处，选项 She might not gain more experience. 把它说成负面后果，方向相反。\n借用原文：I've gathered a lot of experience in different jobs", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What benefit has the woman gained from changing her jobs frequently?", "options": ["A) Experience.", "B) Confidence.", "C) Fortune.", "D) Opportunity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Experience.\n【定位】The way I see it, through having different jobs, I've gathered a lot of experience in different\njobs and in different industries.\n在我看来，通过做不同的工作，我在不同岗位、不同行业里积累了很多经验。\n【信号】I've gathered + experience 的收获自述。她用 I've gathered a lot of experience 这\n一\"动词 gather + 名词 experience\"的搭配，亲口说出频繁换工作的收获，正是 benefit 题\n的答案句。\n【替换】I've gathered a lot of experience ↔ Experience.\ngathered experience → Experience\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nI feel pretty confident that I can sell myself 里 confident 确被念到，但那是她描述自己\n的心态、回应别人对她经验不深的质疑，并不是\"频繁换工作带来的好处\"，选项\nConfidence. 盗了 confident 一词答非所问。\n借用原文：I feel pretty confident that I can sell myself\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音结尾的谚语 Fortune favors the brave 里有 Fortune，但那只是她引用的一句鼓励自\n己的俗话，绝非\"换工作得到的好处\"，选项 Fortune. 正是预读预警过的\"谚语原词\"陷阱。\n借用原文：Fortune favors the brave\nD) 原词陷阱\nan opportunity to progress 里 opportunity 出现过，但那是她抱怨现公司\"看不到晋升机\n会\"，是她缺的东西、不是换工作的收获，选项 Opportunity. 盗用该词反着用。\n借用原文：getting an opportunity to progress", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about being alone?", "options": ["A) It's a horrible feeling.", "B) It can be a blessing.", "C) It's boring and dangerous.", "D) It's the most comfortable state."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） It can be a blessing.\n【定位】In contrast, being alone can actually be a blessing, particularly when you've actively\nchosen it.\n相比之下，独自一人其实可以是一件幸事，尤其是当你主动选择独处的时候。\n【信号】转折 In contrast 后才是真意。前句 Loneliness is a horrible feeling. 讲的是『孤独』的\n坏，紧接着 In contrast, being alone can actually be a blessing 用 In contrast 转折，把\n话头转到『独自一人』并给出正面定性。转折信号之后那句才是对 being alone 的真正评\n价，正是答案句。\n【替换】being alone can actually be a blessing ↔ It can be a blessing.\n近乎同句，只换主语措辞\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt's a horrible feeling. 抄了录音 Loneliness is a horrible feeling. 的原词 horrible\nfeeling，但那句形容的是 loneliness（孤独），不是题目问的 being alone（独自一人）。\n听到 horrible feeling 就勾，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Loneliness is a horrible feeling.\nC) 与原文相反\nIt's boring and dangerous. 借了录音 being bored and alone is dangerous 里的\ndangerous，但原文说的是『又无聊又独处才危险』，是有条件的；并非给 being alone 本\n身定性为危险。把条件去掉直接说独处『就是』无聊危险，与原意不符。\n借用原文：being bored and alone is dangerous and can easily lead to the feeling of loneliness\nD) 过度推断\nIt's the most comfortable state. 用了 the most comfortable 这种最高级，把独处吹成\n『最舒适的状态』。录音只说它可以是 a blessing，从未说它是『最舒适』，属于无中生有\n的拔高。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the speaker say helps reduce one's feeling of loneliness?", "options": ["A) To be active.", "B) To meet up with your friends.", "C) To travel abroad.", "D) To seek advice from others."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） To be active.\n【定位】The trick is to be active.\n诀窍就是让自己动起来、保持活跃。\n【信号】建议句 The trick is to…。前一句说『又无聊又独处会导致孤独感』，紧接着用 The trick\nis to be active. 给出对策。The trick is to 是典型的建议/方法信号，其后的 be active 就是\n说话人开出的药方，是答案句。\n【替换】The trick is to be active ↔ To be active.\n原句核心动作直接对应\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nTo meet up with your friends. 抄了录音 meet up with your friends，但原文说的是『独\n处一阵后再去见朋友，你会更投入』，讲的是见朋友的好时机，不是用来减轻孤独感的办\n法。听到 meet up with friends 就勾，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：when it's time to be social and meet up with your friends, you will be fully there\nC) 未提及\nTo travel abroad. 说出国旅行，录音只讲了 Get outside（出去走走）、do something\ncultural（做点文化活动），从未提到 travel abroad，属于无中生有。\nD) 未提及\nTo seek advice from others. 说向别人求教，听起来合常识，但录音给的对策是 be active\n（自己动起来），全程没说要 seek advice from others，是凭常识硬填的干扰项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is an advantage of spending one's time alone?", "options": ["A) It provides a chance for people to think deeply.", "B) It makes us treasure the time.", "C) It enables one to identify true friends.", "D) It helps us take care of problems more efficiently."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It helps us take care of problems more efficiently.\n【定位】Spending time alone also allows you to more efficiently take care of problems.\n独处还能让你更高效地处理各种问题。\n【信号】列举好处的 also allows you to…。录音用 Spending time alone also allows you to\nmore efficiently take care of problems 直接陈述独处的一项好处，also 表明这是在罗列\n益处中的又一条，allows you to 引出具体好处，是答案句。\n【替换】more efficiently take care of problems ↔ It helps us take care of problems more efficiently.\n几乎原词，仅调整语序\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt provides a chance for people to think deeply. 说独处给人深入思考的机会，听起来合\n理，但录音列举的好处是『高效处理问题、社交时更投入、更珍惜朋友』，从没提 think\ndeeply，属于未提及。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt makes us treasure the time. 蹭了录音结尾 your time spent together will be more\nworthwhile 里的 time，但原文说的是『和朋友共处的时间更值得』，不是『珍惜时间\n（treasure the time）』。借 time 一词偷换概念。\n借用原文：your time spent together will be more worthwhile\nC) 偷换概念\nIt enables one to identify true friends. 蹭了录音 appreciate your friends' company\nmore 里的 friends，但原文说的是独处后『更珍惜朋友的陪伴』，不是『辨别出真朋友\n（identify true friends）』。借 friends 一词偷换概念。\n借用原文：you will also appreciate your friends' company more", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do we learn about the speaker's father?", "options": ["A) He is a harsh person.", "B) He is mean to others.", "C) He is very demanding in his work.", "D) He usually works very late."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He is very demanding in his work.\n【定位】My father wasn't being mean. He demanded the same of himself.\n我父亲并不是刻薄。他对自己也是同样的要求。\n【信号】否定澄清句定调。My father wasn't being mean. 用 wasn't 主动否定'刻薄'这一负面解\n读，紧接着 He demanded the same of himself. 把父亲的真实形象定为'对工作和自己都\n要求高'——这种'先否定误解、再给真意'的句子就是答案信号。\n【替换】anybody who worked for Thomas Kahoon had to meet his standards ↔\nvery demanding in his work\n高标准=要求高\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHe is a harsh person. 的 harsh 在录音里确实出现过，但那是 the harsh acid smell of\nthe cleaning solution 形容清洁剂刺鼻的气味，根本不是说父亲这个人苛刻——典型听到\n原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：the harsh acid smell of the cleaning solution\nB) 与原文相反\nHe is mean to others. 的 mean 录音里出现在 My father wasn't being mean.——是被\nwasn't 否定掉的说法，选它等于把说话人否定的话当成了肯定的结论，方向正好相反。\n借用原文：My father wasn't being mean.\nD) 张冠李戴\nHe usually works very late. 里'待到很晚'对应 he made me stay late until I got it\nright，但那是父亲让'我'留下来重做，加班的是说话人自己，不是父亲；而且这是偶尔(做错\n时)而非 usually(通常)。\n借用原文：he made me stay late until I got it right", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the housing project?", "options": ["A) He moved out and divorced.", "B) He was plagued by drugs and gang violence.", "C) He lived there for 20 years.", "D) His parents would move into his new house."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He was plagued by drugs and gang violence.\n【定位】I moved out of my parents' modest place into a housing project. Drugs and gang violence\nwere just beginning to plague the projects.\n我搬出父母简朴的住处，住进了一个公共住宅区。毒品和帮派暴力当时正开始侵扰这些住\n宅区。\n【信号】定位词后紧跟的描述句。录音一出现定位词 a housing project，下一句立刻 Drugs and\ngang violence were just beginning to plague the projects. 给出对这个地方的直接描述\n——题目问的对象一出现，紧跟其后的那句往往就是答案句。\n【替换】Drugs and gang violence were just beginning to plague the projects ↔\nHe was plagued by drugs and gang violence\n主语换了人但同义\n【排除】A) 未提及\nHe moved out and divorced. 前半'搬走'对应 I got married and moved out，但录音说\n的是结婚后搬出，全篇只字未提 divorced(离婚)；这一半凭空捏造，整项就错。\n借用原文：I got married and moved out\nC) 原词陷阱\nHe lived there for 20 years. 的 20 在录音里确实念到，但 when I was 20 说的是他结婚那\n年20岁，是年龄不是在那住了20年——数字被挪去说时长，对象偷换了。\n借用原文：when I was 20\nD) 与原文相反\nHis parents would move into his new house. 说父母搬进他的新家，而录音是'我'从父母\n处搬出 moved out of my parents' modest place，方向正相反，且录音从没提父母搬\n来。\n借用原文：moved out of my parents' modest place", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do we learn about the speaker as a stock clerk?", "options": ["A) He was only responsible for unloading food.", "B) He had to sign his name on every label.", "C) It was a hard and tedious job.", "D) He was required to work on Friday night."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It was a hard and tedious job.\n【定位】When we married, I worked as a stock clerk at Southwest Super Foods. It was hard,\ntedious work.\n我们结婚时，我在西南超级食品店做理货员。那是又苦又枯燥的活儿。\n【信号】直接的概括评价句。录音点出身份 I worked as a stock clerk 后，紧跟一句 It was hard,\ntedious work. 对这份工作做整体定性——这种对工作'是个什么活儿'的直接概括句，正是\n问'当理货员是什么情况'的答案句。\n【替换】It was hard, tedious work ↔ It was a hard and tedious job\nwork=job 近义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHe was only responsible for unloading food. 的 unload 对应 cases of food that had\nto be unloaded, priced, and placed on shelves，但录音说货物要卸货、定价、上架三件\n事都做，选项用 only 只留卸货，把职责框窄了，与原文不符。\n借用原文：cases of food that had to be unloaded, priced, and placed on shelves\nB) 偷换概念\nHe had to sign his name on every label. 的 label 对应 the labels facing smartly out，\n但录音是说他把罐头标签朝外摆齐、这是他的'招牌(signature 比喻)'，并非真在每个标签上\n签名；把 That was my signature. 的比喻签名当成了字面签名。\n借用原文：the labels facing smartly out\nD) 与原文相反\nHe was required to work on Friday night. 的 Friday night 录音里念过，但原文是别人都\n想请假、I was always ready to work 是他主动乐意上夜班，不是 required(被要求)；把自\n愿说成被强制，性质反了。\n借用原文：I was always ready to work", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "By what means did scientists at University College London measure memory loss?", "options": ["A) By recording the time people spend on TV.", "B) By tracking people's living habits.", "C) By using memory and fluency tests.", "D) By scanning people's brains."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By using memory and fluency tests.\n【定位】Scientists at University College London used memory and fluency tests on the same group of\npeople six years apart.\n伦敦大学学院的科学家们对同一组人，前后相隔六年，做了记忆力与流畅度测试。\n【信号】动作动词 used 紧跟方法。答案句信号是表\"使用某方法\"的动作动词 used memory and\nfluency tests，问\"用什么手段\"的细节题，落点就在这个 used 后面带出的具体工具上。\n【替换】used memory and fluency tests ↔ By using memory and fluency tests.\n原句动词转成 By doing 的近乎直译\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 By recording the time people spend on TV.，借了录音反复出现的 television 话题\n词，但研究测量记忆用的是 memory and fluency tests，不是\"记录看电视的时间\"，这是\n听到 TV 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Watching more than three hours of television a day\nB) 未提及\nB 说 By tracking people's living habits.\"追踪生活习惯\"，录音通篇只提到用 memory\nand fluency tests，从未说追踪生活习惯，属于编造出来的手段。\nD) 未提及\nD 说 By scanning people's brains.，\"扫脑\"听上去很像测记忆的高科技方法，但录音明确\n用的是 memory and fluency tests，从头到尾没有提到 scan 或 brain scanning。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What contributed to memory decline in the participants?", "options": ["A) Watching television for hours.", "B) Playing video games.", "C) Reading books and magazines.", "D) Surfing the Internet."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Watching television for hours.\n【定位】Watching more than three hours of television a day doubles memory loss in older people, a\nnew study of more than 3,000 adults suggests.\n一项针对三千多名成年人的新研究表明，每天看电视超过三小时会使老年人的记忆力损失\n加倍。\n【信号】因果动词 doubles 连起活动与后果。答案句的信号是 doubles memory loss 这个把\"看电\n视\"和\"记忆损失\"直接挂钩的因果动词，问\"什么导致记忆衰退\"，落点就在 doubles 前面的\n那个行为上。\n【替换】Watching more than three hours of television a day ↔ Watching television for hours.\n具体时长概括为 for hours\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nB 说 Playing video games.，录音确实念了 playing video games，但那是作为\"有助于保\n持脑力的活动\"提的，方向是有益、不是导致衰退，听到原词就选会张冠李戴。\n借用原文：interactive screen-based pursuits such as using the Internet or playing video games\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 说 Reading books and magazines.，录音里 reading 是与\"保持脑功能\"绑在一起的有益\n活动，不是导致记忆衰退的原因，借原词反向设套。\n借用原文：activities known to preserve mental functioning such as reading\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 说 Surfing the Internet.，录音 using the Internet 同样是被列为\"有益脑力\"的活动，不\n是诱因，属典型的原词反用。\n借用原文：interactive screen-based pursuits such as using the Internet", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do the researchers say about their finding concerning the link between TV viewing and Alzheimer's disease?", "options": ["A) Television viewing may be a potential factor for Alzheimer's disease.", "B) Alzheimer's patients tend to watch television more than 3 hours a day.", "C) Some research has confirmed the link between them.", "D) Television watching is beneficial to Alzheimer's patients."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Television viewing may be a potential factor for Alzheimer's disease.\n【定位】The researchers say that television viewing may be a risk factor for Alzheimer's disease, but\nmore research is needed to establish a link.\n研究者表示，看电视可能是老年痴呆症的一个风险因素，但还需要更多研究来确立这种\n关联。\n【信号】留余地词 may + 转折 but 限定结论。答案句信号是 may be a risk factor 的不确定词\nmay，以及紧随的转折 but more research is needed，研究者用\"可能、还需更多研究\"把\n话说得留有余地，结论就落在这种谨慎表态上。\n【替换】may be a risk factor for Alzheimer's disease ↔\nTelevision viewing may be a potential factor for Alzheimer's disease.\nrisk factor 同义为 potential factor，may 原样保留\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 说 Alzheimer's patients tend to watch television more than 3 hours a day.，录音确\n有 more than three hours 这个数字，但那是讲\"看电视时长与记忆损失\"，从没说\"老年痴\n呆患者每天看超三小时\"，是借数字原词设的陷阱。\n借用原文：those who tended to watch more than three hours a day\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说 Some research has confirmed the link between them.，但录音明说 more\nresearch is needed to establish a link——关联还没确立，C 用 confirmed 把\"尚未证\n实\"夸成\"已证实\"，程度严重越界。\n借用原文：more research is needed to establish a link\nD) 与原文相反\nD 说 Television watching is beneficial to Alzheimer's patients.，录音里确有\neducational benefits and relaxation benefits，但那是泛指看电视的一般好处，绝没\n说\"对老年痴呆患者有益\"，整体基调是看电视有害，D 完全反向。\n借用原文：watching television may have educational benefits and relaxation benefits", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What do the researchers suggest older people do?", "options": ["A) Watch television no more than 3 hours each day.", "B) Balance television viewing with other contrasting activities.", "C) Watch some educational TV programs.", "D) Take more physical exercise."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Balance television viewing with other contrasting activities.\n【定位】the researchers advise that adults over the age of 50 should try and ensure that television\nviewing is balanced with other contrasting activities.\n研究者建议，五十岁以上的成年人应尽量确保看电视与其他相反类型的活动保持平衡。\n【信号】建议信号词 advise + should。答案句信号是建议词 the researchers advise 加 should\ntry and ensure，问\"建议做什么\"的题，落点就在 advise/should 后面带出的那个动作上。\n【替换】television viewing is balanced with other contrasting activities ↔\nBalance television viewing with other contrasting activities.\n被动陈述转成主动祈使，措辞几乎照搬\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 Watch television no more than 3 hours each day.，录音里 more than three\nhours 确实念过，但那是描述研究发现的时长门槛，研究者给老年人的正式建议是\"平衡活\n动\"，并非\"每天别超三小时\"，借数字原词冒充建议。\n借用原文：Watching more than three hours of television a day\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说 Watch some educational TV programs.，录音确有 educational benefits 一词，但\n那是说看电视\"有教育益处\"，研究者从没建议\"去看教育类节目\"，借 educational 原词偷换\n概念。\n借用原文：watching television may have educational benefits\nD) 未提及\nD 说 Take more physical exercise.，录音结尾只笼统提到 undertake some healthy\nhobbies，从未具体建议\"多做体育锻炼\"，physical exercise 是凭健康常识脑补出来的。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) accessible\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 + 空 + to all，典型表语形容词位\n【依据】be accessible to all = 「人人可参与」契合 Franklin 的科学观\n前句引语 \"Science and everyday life cannot and should not be separated.\" 强调科学\n不该脱离日常生活，自然推出科学的探索应「人人能够参与/接触」，accessible 与 to all\n构成固定搭配，意思精准。\n【竞争词】E) considerable 义为「相当大的」，与 to all 不搭配，逻辑也不通\nconsiderable 修饰程度/数量，不说 considerable to all；且「科学的追求对所有人是相当大\n的」根本不成句。\nF) effective 义为「有效的」，不与 to all 形成自然搭配\neffective 一般搭 for / against / in doing，说 the pursuit should be effective to all 既别扭也\n偏题——本句要谈的不是「有效」而是「向所有人开放」。\nL) senior 义为「高级的」，与「人人」相反\nsenior to sb 虽能搭配，但意为「比某人级别高」，与「面向所有人」语义完全相反；且 senior\n留给 33 题的「senior levels」更贴。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— effective 词性合、感情色彩也对，最易误选。the pursuit\nof science should be __(26)__ to all 中，effective 是形容词、又显得「积极」；但\neffective 后不接 to all（应接 for / in doing），且与 Franklin 引言「科学与日常不可分」\n呼应的是「面向所有人」而非「有效」。防坑：be 形容词 + to all 框架下只挑能搭 to 的形\n容词，accessible 才对。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) overlooked\n动词\n【词性槽】were 后缺表语，且 sadly 修饰，需要过去分词作被动\n【依据】「贡献被忽视」与下一段「幸而我们今天活在不同世界」呼应\nFranklin 是 DNA 结构发现的重要贡献者却长期未被承认；overlooked「被忽视」精准捕捉\n这一史实，且与下文 we are __(28)__ living in a different world 的转折逻辑「过去被忽视\n——如今境况变好」环环相扣。\n【竞争词】H) misread 义为「误读、被错误理解」，偏离史实\nmisread 强调「读错、误解」，但 Franklin 的问题不是贡献被「误读」，而是根本没被承认/记\n得；与 sadly 的情绪虽都偏负面，但语义指向不同。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— misread 形态正确、色彩偏负，迷惑性极高。were sadly\n__(27)__ in her lifetime 中，misread 在形态（过去分词）和情绪（负面）上都过关；但回\n到「下一段是『幸而如今活在不同世界』」的转折，被翻转的应是「被忽视」而非「被误\n读」。防坑：被动+情绪转折结构里，要把上下段两端的语义都摆上桌再选。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) thankfully\n副词\n【词性槽】be 与现在分词之间，只能塞副词\n【依据】评注性副词，与「不同于过去的世界」语义对接\n本句对比的是 Franklin's contributions … were sadly __(27)__ in her lifetime 那种「可惜\n被忽视」的旧时代，到了今天「女性已能在各阶层发挥作用」。作者对这种转变持庆幸态\n度，thankfully「幸亏、值得庆幸地」正好对上这一情绪。\n【竞争词】D) appropriately 意思是「恰当地」，与「活在不同世界」语义不搭\nappropriately 修饰动作，强调「做得合适」；说 we are appropriately living in a different\nworld（我们恰当地活在一个不同的世界）逻辑不通。它更适合修饰「报酬/做法」，留给\n35 题。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) promoting\n动词\n【词性槽】and 并列 advancing 后，要填同为现在分词形式的动词\n【依据】promote gender equality 是英文里高频固定搭配\npromote「推动、促进」与 gender equality 搭配是公共议题文中的标配（promote\nequality），与前面的 advancing 构成同义递进，强化「英国高校在推进性别平等上是世界\n领跑者」。\n【竞争词】N) suggesting 义为「暗示」，与「推动」语义层次不对\nsuggest gender equality「暗示性别平等」逻辑不通，suggest 后更常接 that 从句或 doing；\n此处与 advancing 并列要求的是一个「积极促进」的动作，suggesting 偏弱、偏间接。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) considerable\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 increase 之间，是定语形容词位\n【依据】「相当大的增长」是描述数据上涨最常用的搭配\n本段是数据/事实段，要描写 STEM 女性本科生录取人数过去十年的「大幅」增长，\nconsiderable 修饰 increase 是固定高频搭配（a considerable increase = 相当大的增\n长），与下一句「女性研究生占 STEM 半数以上」相互佐证。\n【竞争词】F) effective 义为「有效的」，不修饰增长的「幅度」\nan effective increase 不是地道搭配；effective 强调效果是否到位，而本句要说的是增长「大不\n大」，错位。effective 更适合修饰措施/政策。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) accounted\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 women 后缺谓语 + for 的搭配信号\n【依据】account for 「占（比例）」与「过半」精准匹配\nwomen __(31)__ for more than half of all Stem postgraduates 表达「女性占 STEM 研\n究生半数以上」，account for 是「占（比例/数量）」的固定搭配；过去时 accounted 也\n匹配 in the last academic year 的时间状语。\n【竞争词】H) misread 义为「误读」，与「占半数」语义南辕北辙\nmisread for 不是固定搭配；说「女性误读了一半研究生」更是语义荒谬。\nJ) overlooked 义为「忽视」，不与 for 搭配，主动语态也不通\noverlook 通常用作及物动词、且本题语境中 women 不会主动「忽视」研究生群体；此外\noverlook for 不成立。\nK) promoting 现在分词形态不能独立作过去时谓语\npromoting 是 -ing 形式，要作谓语需 are/were promoting；这里没助动词，形态不对；且\npromoting for 不是搭配。\nN) suggesting 同样是 -ing 形式，无助动词无法作谓语\nsuggesting 形态与 promoting 同理；且 suggest 的常见结构是 suggest that / suggest\ndoing，与 for 不构成习惯搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) ladder\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 后 + 后接介词短语，必填名词\n【依据】与 climb、the further up 形成形象化搭配「爬阶梯」\n句中 gets harder for women to climb the further up they go 用了 climb（爬）和 the\nfurther up（越往上）这两个动作/方位词，需要一个能被「爬」和「越往上」修饰的具体名\n词；ladder「（事业晋升的）阶梯」恰好和 climb the ladder（攀登事业阶梯）构成习语搭\n配，比喻女性在职业晋升中越往上越难。\n【竞争词】C) adaptation 义为「改编/适应」，与 climb 搭配不成立\nadaptation 是抽象名词，不能被「爬、越往上」修饰，也和「通往成功」的语境没有逻辑\n关联。\nI) nomination 义为「提名」，不能被「攀爬」\nnomination 指被推荐为候选人，是单点动作，不存在「越往上越难爬」这种动态过程。\nM) submission 义为「提交/屈服」，与「通往成功」语义相反\nsubmission 表「屈服、顺从」时带消极色彩，且不能 climb；表「提交（论文/作业）」时也与\n「成功之路」对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) senior\n形容词\n【词性槽】介词 At 后 + 名词 levels 前，是定语形容词位\n【依据】与 professors（教授）形成「高级 / 资深职位」语境\n句子说 At __(33)__ levels, only a quarter of professors are women，是承接上一句「越\n往上越难爬」的论据：在「高级 senior 职位」上，女性教授只占四分之一。senior levels\n（高级别岗位）是固定搭配，与「教授」「学术高层」呼应。\n【竞争词】E) considerable 义为「相当大的」，修饰 levels 语义空洞\nat considerable levels 不是常见搭配，也表达不出「最高层」的语义；与「教授仅四分之一为\n女性」这一具体层级数据对不上。\nF) effective 义为「有效的」，与 levels 搭配偏题\nat effective levels 一般指「在有效（达标）的水平上」，与本句要表达的「在高级别岗位」逻\n辑完全错位。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) suggesting\n动词\n【词性槽】逗号后接独立成分 + there is，是现在分词作伴随状语\n【依据】用数据「表明 / 暗示」结论，是论说文标准搭桥词\nsuggesting 「表明、暗示」连接前句「薪酬差距 13.7%」这一数据与后句「在确保女性晋\n升和同酬上仍有相当长的路要走」的结论，是数据 + 推论的最佳过渡。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessible", "B) accounted", "C) adaptation", "D) appropriately", "E) considerable", "F) effective", "G) ladder", "H) misread", "I) nomination", "J) overlooked", "K) promoting", "L) senior", "M) submission", "N) suggesting", "O) thankfully"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) appropriately\n副词\n【词性槽】动词被动 being paid 后，再补一个状语副词\n【依据】「被恰当地支付」= 同工同酬，正好对应同薪议题\nappropriately 「恰当地、合宜地」用来修饰「报酬」时，指向「与劳动/地位相称的报酬」\n——和上文 13.7% 的性别薪酬差距正反呼应：要让女性「以恰当方式获得报酬」就是缩小\n这一差距。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Cooking benefits people in many ways and enables them to connect with one another.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】做饭让人受益并彼此连接\n【定位】Shouldn't preparing—and consuming—food be a source of comfort, pride, health, well-being,\nrelaxation, sociability? Something that connects us to other humans?\n备饭和吃饭难道不该是安慰、自豪、健康、幸福、放松、社交的来源吗？难道不该是把我们\n与他人连接起来的事情吗？\n【改写】Cooking benefits people in many ways ↔\nShouldn't preparing—and consuming—food be a source of comfort, pride, health, well-being,\nrelaxation, sociability?\n列举换概括\nenables them to connect with one another ↔ Something that connects us to other humans\n同根词替换加彼此的同义\n【辨邻】 E E 段确实出现 share with family and friends，看起来像与他人连接，但 E 段主旨是让\n新手也能做简单家常，不是讲做饭让人彼此连接的多重好处；陈述句 benefits people\nin many ways and enables them to connect with one another 要的是做饭本身的\n多重好处加人际连接这一组合，E 段并未陈述。\nwe need to teach people to cook food that's good enough to share with family and friends", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Abundant information about cooking is available either online or on TV.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】网上和电视上都有大量烹饪信息\n【定位】There are plenty of recipes, how-to videos and cooking classes available to anyone who has a\ncomputer, smartphone or television.\n对任何拥有电脑、智能手机或电视的人来说，食谱、教学视频和烹饪课都唾手可得。\n【改写】Abundant information about cooking ↔\nThere are plenty of recipes, how-to videos and cooking classes\nabundant 等于 plenty of\neither online or on TV ↔ anyone who has a computer, smartphone or television\n线上等于电脑或手机 电视原样", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Young people do less cooking at home than the elderly these days.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】年轻人比老人在家做饭少\n【定位】52% of those 65 or older cook at home five or more times per week; only a third of young\npeople do.\n65 岁及以上的人有 52% 一周在家做饭五次或更多；年轻人只有三分之一会这样。\n【改写】Young people do less cooking at home than the elderly ↔\n52% of those 65 or older cook at home five or more times per week; only a third of young\npeople do\n百分比差值等于比较级\nthese days ↔ A recent Harris poll\nrecent 对应 these days\n【辨邻】 I I 段也出现 only a third of young people 这一相同数字，但它是被引来作年轻人言行\n不一的评论素材，不是用来与老人对比下厨频率；陈述句 Young people do less\ncooking at home than the elderly 要的是老对少的对比来源，这只能出在 F。\nI find it strange that only a third of young people report preparing meals at home regularly", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Cooking skills can be improved with practice.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】厨艺靠练习长进\n【定位】Like any skill, cooking gets easier as you do it more: every time you cook, you advance your\nlevel of skills.\n像任何技能一样，做饭做得越多就越容易：每做一次，你的厨艺都会上一个台阶。\n【改写】Cooking skills can be improved with practice ↔\nthe crisis is one of confidence. And the only remedy for that is practice\npractice 是 remedy 的同义替换\nCooking skills can be improved ↔ every time you cook, you advance your level of skills\nadvance 等于 improve", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "In the mid-20th century, most families ate dinner at home instead of eating out.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】20 世纪中叶大多数家庭在家吃晚饭\n【定位】Back in the 1950s most of us grew up in households where Mom cooked virtually\nevery night.\n回到 1950 年代，我们大多数人在妈妈几乎每晚做饭的家庭长大。\n【改写】In the mid-20th century ↔ Back in the 1950s\n十年是世纪中段的下位\nmost families ate dinner at home instead of eating out ↔ Mom cooked virtually every night\n妈妈每晚做饭 等于 全家在家吃\n【辨邻】 H H 段确实出现 frozen dinners were invented in the '40s 与 televisions became\npopular a decade or so later，年代相邻像是 20 世纪中叶的话题，但 H 段说的是预\n制餐如何成为晚餐主力，恰恰是不在家做饭的开端，与题干 ate dinner at home\ninstead of eating out 完全相反。\npackaged, pre-prepared meals have been what's for dinner", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Even those short of time or money should be encouraged to cook for themselves and their family.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】也要鼓励缺时间缺钱的人自己做饭\n【定位】My mission is to encourage green hands and those lacking time or memory to feed\nthemselves. That means we need modest, realistic expectations, and we need to teach people\nto cook food that's good enough to share with family and friends.\n我的使命是鼓励厨房新手和那些没时间没记忆力的人也能自己做饭吃。这意味着我们需要务\n实的、合理的期待，并且要教人们做出能与家人朋友分享的好饭。\n【改写】Even those short of time or money ↔ those lacking time or memory\nshort of 等于 lacking\nshould be encouraged to cook for themselves and their family ↔\nMy mission is to encourage green hands and those lacking time or memory to feed themselves\n使命转被动陈述加 feed 等于 cook for\n【辨邻】 M M 段确实出现 money is a concern 与自己做的建议，但 M 段重心是购物指南——去\n超市、买真食物、不用本地有机；它没有像 E 段那样把门槛人群作为被鼓励对象、也\n没有点出缺时间这个维度，故不是 Even those short of time or money should be\nencouraged 这种陈述的源头。\nTo those Americans for whom money is a concern, my advice is simple: Buy what you can afford,\nand cook it yourself\nP P 段确实出现 Time 与调整优先级的建议，但它讲的是给所有人挤时间的方法，并未把\n缺时间的人作为一个被鼓励的人群单独点出；陈述句 Even those short of time or\nmoney should be encouraged to cook for themselves and their family 强调的是\n对门槛人群的鼓励，这只出在 E。\nTime, I realize, is the biggest obstacle to cooking for most people. You must adjust your priorities to\nfind time to cook", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Eating food not cooked by ourselves can cause serious consequences.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不自己做饭会有严重后果\n【定位】Considering that the government's standards are not nearly ambitious enough, the picture is\nclear: by not cooking at home, we're not eating the right things, and the consequences are\nhard to overstate.\n考虑到政府标准远不够进取，事情很清楚：因为不在家做饭，我们就吃不对，后果难以\n高估。\n【改写】Eating food not cooked by ourselves ↔ by not cooking at home\n正反同义\ncan cause serious consequences ↔ the consequences are hard to overstate\n难以高估 等于 严重\n【辨邻】 K K 段紧接 J 段，用 burger 作为坏饮食代价的一个具体量化案例，给出 profit from\nburgers is more than offset by the damage they cause in health problems and\nenvironmental harm——这是为 J 段难以高估的后果做的例证，不是严重后果的总判\n词本身；陈述句 Q42 要的是总判词，应回 J。\nthe profit from burgers is more than offset by the damage they cause in health problems and\nenvironmental harm", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "To eat well and still save money, people should buy fresh food and cook it themselves.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】买新鲜食物自己做才省钱又吃好\n【定位】To those Americans for whom money is a concern, my advice is simple: Buy what you can\nafford, and cook it yourself.\n对那些手头紧的美国人，我的建议很简单：买你买得起的，然后自己做。\n【改写】To eat well and still save money ↔\nAnd to save money and still eat well you don't need local, organic ingredients; all you need is\nreal food\n对仗结构直接复现\npeople should buy fresh food and cook it themselves ↔\nBuy what you can afford, and cook it yourself\n祈使转陈述加真食物等于新鲜食物\n【辨邻】 N N 段确实讲自己买、自己做的指南，但它聚焦于 frozen foods 与 canned goods 这\n两个区，不再点 save money 这个维度；陈述句 Q43 的双钉 save money 加 fresh\nfood 在 M 同时齐备，应选 M。\nThe other sections you should get to know are the frozen foods and the canned goods", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "We get a fairly large portion of calories from fast food and snacks.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】我们相当大一部分卡路里来自快餐和零食\n【定位】Nearly two-thirds of us grab fast food once a week, and we get almost 25% of our daily\ncalories from snacks.\n我们近三分之二的人每周吃一次快餐，并且每天约 25% 的卡路里来自零食。\n【改写】a fairly large portion of calories from fast food and snacks ↔\nNearly two-thirds of us grab fast food once a week, and we get almost 25% of our daily\ncalories from snacks\n三词共现加数字证比例", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "The popularity of TV led to the popularity of frozen food.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】电视普及带动冷冻食品流行\n【定位】Although frozen dinners were invented in the '40s, their popularity didn't boom until\ntelevisions became popular a decade or so later.\n虽然冷冻餐在 40 年代就被发明，但要等到大约十年后电视普及，它们才真正风靡。\n【改写】The popularity of TV led to the popularity of frozen food ↔\ntheir popularity didn't boom until televisions became popular a decade or so later\nnot until 因果与 led to 同义\nfrozen food ↔ frozen dinners\nfrozen 同根加 dinners 是 food 的下位\n【辨邻】 G G 段讲 Back in the 1950s 妈妈每晚做饭，时间与 H 段相邻、像是同一年代的话题，\n但 G 段不出现 television 与 frozen 这两个关键锚点；题干 The popularity of TV led\nto the popularity of frozen food 要的是 TV 到 frozen food 的因果链，G 段没有提\n供，不可能是源头段。\nBack in the 1950s most of us grew up in households where Mom cooked virtually every night", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What is happening to the wallet?", "options": ["A) It is disappearing.", "B) It is being fattened.", "C) It is becoming costly.", "D) It is changing in style."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is happening to the wallet 是开放式 what 问句，直接问钱包“正在发生什\n么”，不含 infer/suggest/imply，属于对原文事实的客观转述。\n【拆句】Q46 主干 What is happening to the wallet，what 作主语，is happening 现在进行时，\nto the wallet 介宾短语作状语，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The wallet is heading for extinction. As a day-to-day essential, it will die off with the\ngeneration who read print newspapers.\n钱包正走向消亡。作为日常必需品，它将随着读纸质报纸的那代人一起消失。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nheading for extinction ↔ disappearing\n预判的“正在消失”与 disappearing 在词义、时态上完全吻合，无须再权衡其他选项。\nB) 偷换概念\nbeing fattened 把 fatness 这处比喻钱包鼓胀的怀旧描写当成了事实陈述——原文是\n“过去周五发薪那种鼓胀的感觉令人愉悦”，并不是说钱包真的在变厚。\nC) 无中生有\nbecoming costly 说“钱包变得昂贵”，全文从未讨论钱包本身的售价或制造成本，仅讨\n论它的“消亡”与“象征意义”。\nD) 与原文相反\nchanging in style 暗示钱包还在、只是换了款式，与 heading for extinction 与 It\nmay not be here much longer 表达的“将不复存在”相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "How are business transactions done in big modern stores?", "options": ["A) Individually.", "B) Electronically.", "C) In the abstract.", "D) Via cash register."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】How are business transactions done 用 how 直问交易在大店里怎么完成，要的是原文对\n做法的客观描述。\n【拆句】Q47 主干 transactions are done，被动语态，how 问做法的方式，介词短语 in big\nmodern stores 限定场景。\n【定位】At the most cutting-edge retail stores—Victoria Beckham on Dover Street, for instance—you\ndon't go and stand at any kind of cash register, when you decide to pay. The staff are equipped\nwith iPads to take your payment while you relax on a sofa.\n在最前沿的零售店里——比如多佛街上的维多利亚·贝克汉姆店——付款时你根本不用去站\n在任何收银台前。店员配备了 iPad，你舒服地坐在沙发上，他们就给你结账。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nIndividually 意为“一对一/单独地”，原文虽提到坐在沙发上接受服务的舒适感，但并未\n把这种服务方式称为 individually，也未与“群体性服务”对比。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThe staff are equipped with iPads to take your payment ↔ Electronically\n预判“用电子设备付款”恰好被 Electronically 这一上位副词完整覆盖，定位与替换两端\n都通。\nC) 答非所问\nIn the abstract 取自 the abstraction of the idea of cash，那是作者对现象的评价\n（金钱概念被抽象化），并非交易“如何完成”的具体做法。\nD) 与原文相反\nVia cash register 说“通过收银台”，但定位句明说 you don't go and stand at any\nkind of cash register——大店里恰恰不用收银台。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What makes the author feel uncomfortable nowadays?", "options": ["A) Saving money is becoming a thing of the past.", "B) The pleasing Friday-night feeling is fading.", "C) Earning money is getting more difficult.", "D) Spending money is so fast and easy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What makes the author feel uncomfortable 直问导致作者不适的事物，答案在原文中有\n对应陈述，不需推断。\n【拆句】Q48 主干 What makes the author feel uncomfortable，使役结构 make sb do，what\n作主语问主语本身，nowadays 时间状语限定范围。\n【定位】But across society, the abstraction of the idea of cash makes me uneasy. Maybe I'm just old-\nfashioned. But earning money isn't quick or easy for most of us. Isn't it a bit weird that\nspending it should happen in half a blink of an eye?\n但放眼整个社会，金钱概念被抽象化这件事让我不安。也许我只是老派。但挣钱对大多数人\n来说既不快也不轻松。花钱却能在眨眼之间完成，这难道不有点怪吗？\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nSaving money is becoming a thing of the past 说“存钱在变成过去式”，原文从未讨\n论存钱（saving），只讨论花钱与挣钱。\nB) 张冠李戴\nThe pleasing Friday-night feeling is fading 取自 that time-honoured Friday-night\nfeeling of pleasing, promising fatness，但原文用这一感受作“钱包代表着重要的东西”\n的论据，不是作者声称的不安根源；他不安的是 spending it should happen in half a\nblink of an eye。\nC) 偷换概念\nEarning money is getting more difficult 把原文 earning money isn't quick or easy\nfor most of us 的“挣钱本来就不快不易”误读成“挣钱正变得更难”——原文没有“更难\n（在变化中）”的意思，且作者不舒服的真正对象是花钱、不是挣钱。\nD) ✓ 正确\nspending it should happen in half a blink of an eye ↔ Spending money is so fast and easy\n预判“花钱太快太轻易”与 Spending money is so fast and easy 字面一一对应，不需要\n权衡其他选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why does the author choose to write about what's happening to the wallet?", "options": ["A) It represents a change in the modern world.", "B) It has something to do with everybody's life.", "C) It marks the end of a time-honoured tradition.", "D) It is the concern of contemporary economists."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】Why does the author choose to write 问的是作者的写作动机，原文不会一字一句写“我\n写本文是因为…”，需要从作者明确表达“在意什么”的句子做一步延伸。\n【拆句】Q49 主干 Why does the author choose to write about X，choose to + 不定式作宾\n语，about 引导写作话题，what's happening to the wallet 名词性从句作 about 的\n宾语。\n【定位】But I'll leave the economics to the experts. What bothers me about the death of the wallet is\nthe change it represents in our physical environment.\n但我把经济这层留给专家。真正困扰我的，是钱包消亡所代表的、我们身处的物理环境的\n变化。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe change it represents in our physical environment ↔\nrepresents a change in the modern world\n预判“钱包代表着当代世界的某种变化”与 It represents a change in the modern world\n完全对应。\nB) 过度推断\nhas something to do with everybody's life 说钱包消失与“每个人的生活”都相关，原\n文虽然提到 across society，但作者强调的是“我真正在意的是它代表的变化”，并不是\n“它和每个人有关”。\nC) 以偏概全\nIt marks the end of a time-honoured tradition 取自 that time-honoured Friday-\nnight feeling，但 time-honoured 只是描述周五夜感受的形容词，全文并不是把钱包\n写作“一种古老传统的终结”，而是借它说环境变化。\nD) 与原文相反\nconcern of contemporary economists 说钱包消失是当代经济学家关心的事，但作者\n在定位句开头就写明 I'll leave the economics to the experts，把经济视角主动让出去\n——这正是他写本文的动机所排除的对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What can we infer from the passage about the author?", "options": ["A) He is resistant to social changes.", "B) He is against technological progress.", "C) He feels reluctant to part with the traditional wallet.", "D) He feels insecure in the ever-changing modern world."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】题干 What can we infer from the passage about the author 直接用 infer 提示推断题，\n要求基于全文判断作者形象。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 What can we infer，情态动词 can 表能力可能，from the passage 与 about\nthe author 是两个并列介宾短语分别交代依据与对象，结构平稳无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nresistant to social changes 说作者抗拒“社会变化”——但作者 Maybe I'm just old-\nfashioned 已自嘲只是老派，且 I'll leave the economics to the experts 表明他不打算\n就更大范围的社会议题表态。\nB) 与原文相反\nagainst technological progress 说作者反对科技进步，但作者明确称 Which is\nnothing more or less than excellent service——他承认电子化是优秀服务；他不满的\n是钱包消亡所代表的物理环境变化，而非科技本身。\nC) ✓ 正确\nEverything about the look and feel of a wallet—the way the fastenings and materials wear and\ntear and loosen with age, the plastic and paper and gold and silver, and handwritten phone\nnumbers and printed cinema tickets—is the very opposite of what our world is becoming\n↔ reluctant to part with the traditional wallet\nShow your wallet, if you still have one. It may not be here much longer ↔\nreluctant to part with the traditional wallet\n三段表态聚合出“对传统钱包眷恋而不舍”，与 He feels reluctant to part with the\ntraditional wallet 完全吻合。\nD) 无中生有\nfeels insecure in the ever-changing modern world 说作者在“日新月异的现代世界感\n到不安全”，原文里作者表达的是不安（uneasy）与困扰（bothers me），均针对钱包\n消亡这一具体事，没有任何对“安全感缺失”的描述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is scientists' finding about dreaming?", "options": ["A) It involves disconnected weird images.", "B) It resembles fragments of science fiction.", "C) Dreaming about a learned task betters its performance.", "D) Dreaming about things being learned disturbs one's sleep."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干What is scientists' finding about dreaming是“what is …finding”句式，问一个具体\n事实，不含 infer/imply 等推断信号，也不问 mainly about/title，属常规细节题。\n【拆句】What is scientists' finding about dreaming? 主干是 What is scientists' finding，about\ndreaming 是介词短语作 finding 的后置定语，限定“关于做梦的发现”。句子结构简单，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Scientists have found that dreaming about a task we've learned is associated with improved\nperformance in that activity\n科学家发现，梦见我们学过的某项任务与该任务表现的提升相关联。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nIt involves disconnected weird images只是the fragmented, often bizarre\nimaginings they generate at night的转述，是“梦的现象”而非“科学家的发现”，与题干\n所问的 finding 不在同一语义层。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt resembles fragments of science fiction借It might sound like science fiction这句\n比喻造干扰；原文只是说“听起来像科幻小说”，并不是科学家的发现内容。\nC) ✓ 正确\ndreaming about a task we've learned ↔ Dreaming about a learned task\nis associated with improved performance in that activity ↔ betters its performance\n预判要点“梦到所学任务→表现提升”，Dreaming about a learned task betters its\nperformance恰好覆盖“梦到所学任务”和“表现提升”两个语义成分，与定位句完全对位。\nD) 无中生有\n原文只谈梦对学习的“正向作用”，从未提及Dreaming about things being learned\ndisturbs one's sleep中所说的“扰乱睡眠”这一层；属凭空增加结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What happens when one enters a dream state?", "options": ["A) The body continues to act as if the sleeper were awake.", "B) The neural activity of the brain will become intensified.", "C) The brain behaves as if it were playing a virtual reality video game.", "D) The brain once again experiences the learning activities of the day."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干What happens when one enters a dream state问“进入梦境时发生什么”，是对客观\n事实的提问，不含推断信号。\n【拆句】What happens when one enters a dream state? 主句 What happens 是主语从句疑问形\n式，when 引导时间状语从句修饰 happens，one 是泛指代词。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】While we sleep, research indicates, the brain replays the patterns of activity it experienced\nduring waking hours, allowing us to enter what one psychologist calls a neural(神经的)\nvirtual reality.\n研究表明，当我们睡觉时，大脑会重演它在清醒时所经历的活动模式，从而让我们进入某心\n理学家所称的“神经虚拟现实”。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nThe body continues to act as if the sleeper were awake只描述了patients with\nconditions like sleepwalking, in which the sleeper engages in the kind of physical\nmovement that does not normally occur during sleep这一梦游异常情况；原文明示\n这并非常态。\nB) 无中生有\nThe neural activity of the brain will become intensified借neural(神经的) virtual\nreality中的 neural 一词造形似干扰；原文只说大脑重演活动，从未说神经活动“变得更\n强烈”。\nC) 过度推断\nThe brain behaves as if it were playing a virtual reality video game把原文比喻\nwhat one psychologist calls a neural(神经的) virtual reality扩写为“在玩虚拟现实电\n子游戏”，添加了 game 这一无中生有的设定。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe brain replays ↔ The brain once again experiences\nthe patterns of activity it experienced during waking hours ↔\nthe learning activities of the day\n预判核心“大脑再次经历白天的活动”，The brain once again experiences the\nlearning activities of the day在“再次”“经历”“白天活动”三处均精确等值。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the brain do while we are sleeping?", "options": ["A) It systematizes all the data collected during the day.", "B) It substitutes old information with new data.", "C) It processes and absorbs newly acquired data.", "D) It classifies information and places it in different files."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干What does the brain do while we are sleeping是 what does X do 句式，问大脑在\n做什么这一客观行为。\n【拆句】What does the brain do while we are sleeping? 主句 What does the brain do 是疑问\n句，while we are sleeping 是时间状语从句，sleeping 是现在分词。无陷阱。\n【定位】This shows that while our bodies are at rest, our brains are drawing what's important from the\ninformation and events we've recently encountered, then integrating that data into the vast\nstore of what we already know.\n这表明，当我们的身体休息时，大脑在从最近接触到的信息和事件中提取重要内容，然后把\n这些数据整合进我们已有的庞大知识库中。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nIt systematizes all the data collected during the day把原文what's important（重要\n的部分）擅自扩大为 all the data（全部数据），与原文“筛取重要”相矛盾。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt substitutes old information with new data说“用新数据替代旧信息”，与定位句\nintegrating that data into the vast store of what we already know恰好相反——原\n文是把新数据“整合进”已有知识，并非“替换掉”已有知识。\nC) ✓ 正确\ndrawing what's important from the information and events we've recently encountered ↔\nIt processes and absorbs newly acquired data\nthe information and events we've recently encountered ↔ newly acquired data\n预判核心“提取+整合新信息”，It processes and absorbs newly acquired data两个谓\n语动词精确对应定位句的两个动名词，是最贴合的同义替换。\nD) 无中生有\nIt classifies information and places it in different files中的 classifies（分类）+\nplaces…in different files（放入不同文件）在原文中找不到对应；原文只说“提取重要\n+整合进已有知识库”，没有“分门别类放进不同档案”这一层动作。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does Robert Stickgold suggest about enhancing learning?", "options": ["A) Having a little sleep after studying in the day.", "B) Staying up late before going to bed.", "C) Having a dream about anything.", "D) Thinking about the odds of dreaming about the material."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干What does Robert Stickgold suggest about enhancing learning问某人具体建议什\n么，是事实型问题。\n【拆句】What does Robert Stickgold suggest about enhancing learning? 主干 What does\nRobert Stickgold suggest，about enhancing learning 介词短语作 suggest 的状语，限\n定话题范围。enhancing 是动名词。无陷阱。\n【定位】Robert Stickgold, one of the Harvard researchers, suggests that studying right before bedtime\nor taking a nap following a study session in the afternoon might increase the odds of\ndreaming about the material.\n哈佛研究人员之一的 Robert Stickgold 建议：临睡前学习，或者在下午学习之后小睡一会\n儿，可能会增加梦到所学内容的几率。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ntaking a nap following a study session in the afternoon ↔\nHaving a little sleep after studying in the day\nStickgold 的建议含两条，其中“下午学完后小睡”一条与Having a little sleep after\nstudying in the day逐词对位；选项不必复述全部建议，只要属于建议之一即正确。\nB) 偷换概念\nStaying up late before going to bed（睡前熬夜）与原文studying right before\nbedtime（临睡前学习）方向完全不同——原文建议的是睡前“学习”，不是“熬夜”。\nC) 以偏概全\nHaving a dream about anything说“做关于任何事的梦”，而原文dreaming about the\nmaterial强调“梦到所学的材料”——选项把对象 the material 扩大成 anything，与原意\n不符。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThinking about the odds of dreaming about the material借用原文最后一句Think\nabout that as your head hits the pillow tonight并改造为“去想梦到材料的几率”，但\n原文这句是给读者的提醒，不是 Stickgold 的“增强学习”建议；同时把 odds 这一名词强\n行说成 Stickgold 建议的“做法”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What can be inferred about dreaming from the passage?", "options": ["A) We may enhance our learning through dreaming.", "B) Dreaming improves your language ability.", "C) All sleepwalkers perform dance moves when they are sleeping.", "D) Taking a nap after learning can help you find the way through the maze."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】题干What can be inferred about dreaming from the passage中 inferred 显式宣告本题\n问“从全文可推出”，不是问字面陈述。\n【拆句】What can be inferred about dreaming from the passage? 主干是 What can be\ninferred，be inferred 是被动语态（情态动词 can+be+过去分词），about dreaming 与\nfrom the passage 都是介词短语作状语，分别限定推断对象和推断依据来源。被动结构提\n示考生关注“可被推出来的内容”，而非原文字面说法。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ndreaming is an essential part of understanding, organizing and retaining what we learn ↔\nWe may enhance our learning through dreaming\ncollege students who dreamed about a computer maze(迷宫) task they had learned showed a 10-\nfold improvement in their ability to find their way through the maze compared with participants\nwho did not dream about the task\n↔ We may enhance our learning through dreaming\n全文研究结论、机制、实验、建议四层证据全部指向“做梦有助于学习”，We may\nenhance our learning through dreaming恰好是这条主线的一步合理外推，且情态动\n词 may 留出合理余地，最稳。\nB) 以偏概全\nDreaming improves your language ability只对应原文括号里举的语言例证we're\n\"getting\" a foreign language once we begin dreaming in it，不能覆盖迷宫实验、\nStickgold 建议等其他区间；且原文用 popular notion（流行观念）描述此说法，并非\n作者断言。\nC) 以偏概全\nAll sleepwalkers perform dance moves when they are sleeping把原文one female\npatient on the tape performs the dance moves she learned earlier中“某位女患者”\n扩大为“所有梦游者”，把单一例证拔到全称命题。\nD) 强加因果\nTaking a nap after learning can help you find the way through the maze强行把第\n四段哈佛迷宫实验与第五段 Stickgold 的小睡建议拼成因果关系——原文中两者只是分\n别论证“做梦助学”这一更大主线，并未明示“学完小睡就能走出迷宫”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
